# Detection rules

## Layout
```
detections/
  wazuh/rules/        sentinelforge_rules.xml (or one file per category)
  wazuh/decoders/     custom decoders if needed
  sigma/              one .yml per detection
  metadata/           one .yml per detection (the source of truth for the library page)
tests/logs/           sample log lines per detection, used with wazuh-logtest
tests/detections/     python tests for metadata, IDs, MITRE format
hunting/              one .yml per saved hunt
```

## Metadata (`detections/metadata/SF-XXX.yml`)
Required fields:
- `id: SF-001`
- `name`
- `description`
- `severity: low|medium|high|critical`
- `mitre: [T1110.003]` (validate format `T\d{4}(\.\d{3})?`)
- `telemetry: [windows_security, sysmon, powershell, linux_auth, auditd, suricata]`
- `wazuh_rule_ids: [100100]`
- `sigma_file`
- `version`
- `status` (one of the 5 validation statuses)
- `windows_validation_required: true|false`
- `false_positives`
- `tuning_notes`
- `test_procedure`
- `expected_event`
- `expected_alert`
- `observed_result: "NOT YET VALIDATED"` until a lab test is done

## Wazuh rules
- Custom rule IDs use 100000–109999 only. They must be unique; CI checks for duplicates.
- Group by category:
  - 100100 auth
  - 100200 powershell
  - 100300 accounts/groups
  - 100400 persistence
  - 100500 lateral movement
  - 100600 credential access
  - 100700 linux
  - 100800 defense evasion
  - 100900 network
  - 101000 correlation
- Every rule needs `<description>` with an `SF-XXX` prefix, `<mitre><id>`, and `<group>`.
- Build on Wazuh's built-in parent rules via `if_sid`/`if_group` where possible.
  - Mark parent IDs you are unsure of with `<!-- VERIFY: -->`.
- Correlation uses `frequency`, `timeframe`, `if_matched_sid`, and `same_field` where the Wazuh
  version supports them; otherwise do it in the backend.
- Windows event field names (e.g. `win.eventdata.*`) must be marked `REQUIRES WINDOWS VALIDATION`.

## Sample logs
- Sample log lines in `tests/logs/` must be labeled as synthetic test input.
- A detection reaches `LOGTEST VALID` only after `wazuh-logtest` output is actually captured.

## Sigma
- Use the standard Sigma format with logsource, detection, condition, and level.
- Use ATT&CK tags in the form `attack.t1059.001`.
- Each rule must convert with `sigma convert` (any free backend) in CI.

## Hunts (`hunting/*.yml`)
Required fields:
- name
- hypothesis
- data_source
- query (OpenSearch DSL or query string against `wazuh-archives-*`/`wazuh-alerts-*`)
- expected_behavior
- suspicious_behavior
- mitre
- investigation_guidance
- status

## CI (GitHub Actions)
CI must check:
- YAML lint
- XML well-formedness
- Required metadata fields
- MITRE ID format
- `SF-XXX` naming
- Duplicate Wazuh rule IDs
- Sigma conversion
- pytest

Never claim CI passes until I report a green run.
